#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-38.png", width: 454pt), 70.5pt, 133.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.08pt, 804pt, "38 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(106.92pt, 82.08pt, "Desalination process  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(106.32pt, 105pt, "The thoroughly characterized membranes were utilized for the desalination of a 0.5 M ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 132.6pt, "NaCl solution, which closely mimicked seawater salinity [99]. Similar to the pure water fluxes, ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 160.2pt, "the membranes during the desalination process possessed a stable flux over the entire ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 187.8pt, "experiment (Fig. 13). As in the case of the prior tests, the measurements were done at various ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 215.4pt, "driving forces to thoroughly test the membranes, ensuring a comprehensive analysis of their ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 242.88pt, "performance. The disparity in permeate flux between salty water and pure water can be ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 270.48pt, "attributed to the principle of MD, where only solvent vapors can permeate through the ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 298.08pt, "hydrophobic porous structure of the membrane (Fig. 13). This reduction in transport is governed ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 325.68pt, "by Raoult's law. In membrane distillation, the transport of solvent vapors is directly linked to ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 353.28pt, "the difference in water vapor pressure between the feed and permeate sides. It is important to ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 380.88pt, "note that, regardless of the feed type, an enhancement in transport features is observed with an ")
  #line-at(70.92pt, 408.48pt, "increase in driving force (Fig. 13). ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.92pt, 700.68pt, "Fig. 13. ")
